{
  // ============================================================
  // FILE: backend/tsconfig.seed.json
  // CONSTRUCTION ORDER: #4
  // HOW: Created manually, immediately after tsconfig.json.
  // WHY: The main tsconfig.json only includes "src/**/*" — it completely
  //      ignores everything in prisma/. The seed script lives at
  //      prisma/seed.ts (outside src/). If we ran ts-node with the
  //      main tsconfig, it would refuse to compile seed.ts because
  //      it's outside rootDir ("./src").
  //
  //      This file solves that by extending the main config and
  //      overriding only the parts that are different for the seed script.
  //
  // HOW IT IS USED:
  //      `npm run db:seed` calls:
  //      `ts-node --project tsconfig.seed.json prisma/seed.ts`
  //      The --project flag tells ts-node to use THIS config instead
  //      of the default tsconfig.json.
  // ============================================================
  // "extends" — inherits ALL settings from tsconfig.json.
  // This means we get: strict, esModuleInterop, skipLibCheck, etc. for free.
  // We only need to override the parts that differ.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    // Override "module" to explicitly use commonjs.
    // The seed script uses require()-style module resolution at runtime
    // because ts-node runs it in a CommonJS context.
    // (This was already "commonjs" in the parent, but explicit is clear.)
    "module": "commonjs",
    // Override "outDir" to a separate folder.
    // If the seed script were ever compiled to disk (it normally isn't —
    // ts-node compiles it in memory), it goes to dist-seed/ instead of dist/.
    // This prevents seed compilation output from mixing with production output.
    // dist-seed/ is listed in .gitignore.
    "outDir": "./dist-seed"
  },
  // Override "include" to point ONLY at the seed script.
  // This is the key change — it tells TypeScript to include prisma/seed.ts
  // even though it is outside the src/ rootDir.
  // The main tsconfig's "include": ["src/**/*"] is completely replaced here.
  "include": [
    "prisma/seed.ts"
  ]
}
//<----- ' outDir": "./dist-seed ' -- & -- ' /dist-seed Dir ' ------>
/* `dist-seed/` does not exist yet — and that is completely normal.

`outDir` in a TypeScript config is not a folder that needs to exist beforehand. It is just an instruction: **"if you ever compile this file to disk, put the output here."**

The seed script is run with `ts-node`, which compiles TypeScript **in memory** — it never writes `.js` files to disk at all. So `dist-seed/` is never actually created unless you explicitly run `tsc --project tsconfig.seed.json`, which nobody does in normal workflow.

The folder only appears if:
- You manually run `tsc --project tsconfig.seed.json`
- Some unusual build pipeline compiles the seed script ahead of time

In the normal flow (`npm run db:seed`), the command is:
```
ts-node --project tsconfig.seed.json prisma/seed.ts
```

`ts-node` reads the tsconfig to understand the TypeScript settings (strict mode, module format, etc.), compiles the file in memory, runs it immediately, and discards the compiled output. No `dist-seed/` folder ever appears.

That is why `.gitignore` lists `backend/dist-seed/` — not because it currently exists, but as a precaution so that if it ever does get created accidentally, it won't be committed to git.

**Summary:** `dist-seed/` is a declared output location that is never actually used in this project's workflow. Its presence in `tsconfig.seed.json` is correct but the folder itself should not exist on disk.

